CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure tracks a dataset's representation through a vision transformer as a vertical stack of five flat square planes in isometric perspective, with an architecture legend up the left side. The legend alternates upward-pointing arrows with four rounded boxes, each reading ViT block x3, so consecutive planes are separated by three transformer blocks. Every plane carries the same set of plotted points, each colored by its semantic class in roughly ten hues including teal, blue, olive, green, orange, red, brown, purple, and pink. Dashed curves in matching colors connect each point to its counterpart on the plane above, so one image can be followed through the whole stack. On the bottom plane the colors are thoroughly mixed within a narrow band; the middle planes remain largely intermingled; and by the top plane the points have spread out into separate compact clumps, each a single color. Position encodes the projected embedding and color encodes class, so what the reader compares plane to plane is how tangled the colors are. The coordinates come from a t-SNE projection computed jointly across the layers shown, giving a rough view of how depth disentangles the classes.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Five identical flat square planes stacked vertically, drawn in isometric so each reads as a diamond outline
  - Dark charcoal border stroke around each plane (thin, ~2px equivalent)
  - Faint light-gray cross gridlines (one horizontal, one vertical mid-line) drawn inside each plane
  - Scatter of ~250 small filled circular points on every plane, identical point set repeated plane to plane, colored by semantic class in ~10 tab10-style hues
  - Dashed colored curves connecting each point to its counterpart on the plane immediately above, forming a dense ribbon bundle between consecutive planes
  - Bottom plane point layout: all classes thoroughly intermixed inside one narrow horizontal band near the plane center
  - Planes 2 and 3 (lower-middle, middle): still largely intermingled, band beginning to thicken and bulge
  - Plane 4: partial separation, some hues starting to pull apart into lobes
  - Top plane: points spread out into ~10 separate compact single-color clumps distributed around the plane
  - Left-side architecture legend column: four rounded-rectangle boxes labeled 'ViT block x3', black outline, white fill, monospace text
  - Upward-pointing black arrows between and above/below the legend boxes (five arrow segments total), forming a vertical flow from bottom to top
  - Vertical alignment: legend column sits at the left, plane stack at the right, each legend box vertically centered between two consecutive planes

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: each plane is a 6 × 6 unit square in the XZ ground plane (X = screen-right-ish, Z = receding diagonal), centered on the Y axis; planes at Y = 0, 3, 6, 9, 12 (total stack height 12). Plane outline stroke #3b3b3b, gridlines #d8d8d8 (two mid-lines crossing at plane center), plane fill white/transparent. Points: radius ~0.06 units, 10 classes × 25 points = 250 points, tab10 palette — teal #17becf, blue #1f77b4, olive #bcbd22, green #2ca02c, orange #ff7f0e, red #d62728, brown #8c564b, purple #9467bd, pink #e377c2, gray #7f7f7f. Same 250 point identities appear on every plane; only their (x,z) coordinates change with depth. Layout read from the source: plane 0 (bottom) — all classes randomly interleaved inside a band spanning x ∈ [-2, 2], z ∈ [-0.6, 0.4] centered slightly below plane center; planes 1 and 2 — same band, widened to x ∈ [-2.4, 2.4] and z ∈ [-1.0, 0.8], colors still interleaved with only small same-color micro-clusters; plane 3 — band splits into 3-4 loose color lobes, cool hues (olive/blue/green/teal) drifting to negative x and warm hues (orange/red/brown/purple/pink) to positive x; plane 4 (top) — ten disjoint clumps each ~0.7 units across, placed left-to-right in this order across the plane: olive (x -2.3, z +0.8), blue (x -2.0, z +0.2), green (x -1.5, z -0.4), teal (x -0.9, z -0.8), orange (x +0.1, z -1.2), gray (x +0.5, z -0.9), brown (x +0.9, z -1.0), red (x +1.2, z -1.1), purple (x +1.6, z -0.9), pink (x +2.2, z -0.6). Connector curves: dashed (dash ~0.12, gap ~0.10), line width ~1, same color as endpoints, alpha ~0.45, drawn as a gentle cubic spline between the point on plane k and the same point on plane k+1 (mostly vertical, fanning outward near the top). Legend column: placed at x ≈ -7.5 in the same world, four rounded boxes (corner radius ~0.35) of size 4.4 × 1.1 units, black 0.06-unit outline, white fill, monospace label 'ViT block x3', box centers at Y = 1.5, 4.5, 7.5, 10.5 (i.e. midway between consecutive planes); five black arrows of length ~1.4 with solid triangular heads pointing +Y, at Y ≈ 0.3, 3.0, 6.0, 9.0, 12.0, connecting the boxes into one vertical flow.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "depth",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "That representations move continuously along the dashed trajectories, and that class colors get progressively less tangled the deeper you go in the network",
    "label": "Depth (embedding → final plane)",
    "range": [
      0,
      4,
      0.05
    ],
    "default": 0
  },
  {
    "id": "stage",
    "type": "state",
    "teaches": "That each jump between planes is three stacked ViT blocks, and that separation accumulates one block-group at a time rather than appearing at once",
    "label": "Step through ViT block x3 groups",
    "states": [
      "embedding",
      "blocks 1-3",
      "blocks 4-6",
      "blocks 7-9",
      "blocks 10-12"
    ],
    "default": "embedding"
  },
  {
    "id": "hoverClass",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "That a single semantic class occupies a coherent trajectory through the stack — scattered in the bottom band, gathered into one clump at the top"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitStack",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "That the planes are genuinely 2D slices stacked in depth, so apparent overlap of clumps is a projection artifact, not true mixing"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Patch embeddings",
    "narration": "At the bottom plane every class color is smeared through one narrow band — the raw embeddings carry almost no linear class separation.",
    "state": {
      "depth": 0,
      "stage": "embedding"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "After 3 ViT blocks",
    "narration": "The first group of three transformer blocks nudges points along their dashed paths; the band thickens but the hues are still heavily intermingled.",
    "state": {
      "depth": 1,
      "stage": "blocks 1-3"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Midway through the network",
    "narration": "Six and nine blocks in, coherent lobes of a single color begin to bulge out of the cloud while most of it remains mixed.",
    "state": {
      "depth": 2.5,
      "stage": "blocks 4-6"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Deep layers pull classes apart",
    "narration": "By the fourth plane the trajectories fan outward and each color is clearly heading for its own region.",
    "state": {
      "depth": 3,
      "stage": "blocks 7-9"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Final representation",
    "narration": "On the top plane the ten classes sit as ten compact, single-colored clumps — the network has untangled the distribution. Hover a clump to trace that class all the way back down.",
    "state": {
      "depth": 4,
      "stage": "blocks 10-12"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render planes as THREE.PlaneGeometry rotated -90° about X with transparent material plus an EdgesGeometry/LineSegments border and two thin LineSegments gridlines. Points: one THREE.Points per class (10 draw calls) with a circular sprite texture and vertexColors off, so hover can dim/brighten whole classes by tweaking material.opacity/size. Dashed connectors: LineSegments with LineDashedMaterial and computeLineDistances(), one merged geometry per class so hover toggles a single object. The depth slider interpolates point positions per-vertex between the stored per-plane coordinate arrays (catmull-rom through the 5 layer positions) and updates the BufferAttribute in place; planes above the current depth fade toward 12% opacity. The stage button snaps depth to integer values and pulses the emissive/opacity of the target plane's border plus the incoming connector bundle. Hover: raycast against the Points clouds with a generous threshold (~0.15); on hit, set all other classes to alpha 0.08 and the hovered class to alpha 1.0 across every plane, and show an HTML tooltip with the class name. Use an OrthographicCamera for the isometric look with OrbitControls (enablePan false, polar angle clamped 30°-80°) and a 'reset view' snap back to the canonical isometric pose. Legend boxes and arrows are best drawn as an HTML/CSS overlay column pinned to the left of the canvas so the monospace type stays crisp, or as Line2 shapes + SDF text in a second orthographic overlay scene.

Follow the interaction plan above.

